Z||000000||RobertVol||09-27-2000||07:32 PM||||I replaced the rear calipers on my 88Gt a few months ago and now after I am having problems with the break pedal going to the floor. (see master cylinder post) <P>But I got a master cylinder to replace on the car and when they were installing the cylinder they came back and said there was ALSO a problem with the caliper on the left rear. Now it will be replaced since still under warranty. BUt what I am wondering is couldnt the problem have been the caliper issue without replacing the master cylinder? Maybe they misdiagnosed the problem and replaced the MAster cylinder and THEN found the problem. <P>So the question is if you have a faulty caliper isnt that enough to cause the pedal to go to the floor?  <P>------------------<BR>Robert<BR>88GT Top 3.8 SC t top <BR>88GT T top 55k for sale<BR>  <A HREF="http://fullspeed.to/NorthFLFieros" TARGET=_blank>http://fullspeed.to/NorthFLFieros</A>  <BR>          <IMG SRC="http://www.fortunecity.com/marina/strand/1086/3eab6a20.jpg"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||RobertVol||09-27-2000||07:36 PM||||ALSO <P>IF the caliper is bad. Were would you suggest getting the best replacement caliper? ||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||09-27-2000||08:11 PM||||Depends how bad the caliper is.<P>The caliper may have been poorly rebuilt or there was trash in the system that ate it up fast.<P>My rule when replacing a MC is to blead the entire system untill I get clean fluid out all 4 wheels. If I'm replacing calipers I bleed clean fluid out the line first to wash away all the crud before the new calipers catch it.<P>flushing clean fluid out the line gets rid of trash and also gets rid of the old fluid which likely has water in it.||||reg||1
Z||000003||RobertVol||09-27-2000||09:07 PM||||Ogre,<P>The calipers that were put back on the car were replaced by someone I trust at a large shop here in FL. I'm positive that the calipers were replaced correctly. I dont know if the caliper had a defect though. But thats my point if defective would that not cause the problem with pedal issue. Not the Master Cylinder ?   ||||reg||1
Z||000004||RobertVol||09-27-2000||09:09 PM||||I am starting to think that may have been the problem and we did not NEED to replace the master cylinder ||||reg||1
